\f3 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f4\b\fs36 The chair-throwing construction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0 Remember our phase-indexed three-space:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ X_\\alpha\\times_7Y_\\beta=Z_\{\\alpha+\\beta\}. \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Now don't merely rotate three directions.\
Build an entire surface from them.\
Define a map into your real seven-dimensional space:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ F(u,\\alpha,\\beta) = u\\tau +r_xX_\\alpha +r_yY_\\beta +r_zZ_\{\\alpha+\\beta\} \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Here \\(u\\) is a real coordinate along the distinguished direction, and \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) are periodic angles.\
So the underlying parameter space is\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb T^2.\} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 We have constructed an object living in \\(\\mathbb R^7\\) whose two phase coordinates generate three rotating coordinate pairs.\
And the third phase isn't independently chosen.\
The cross-product composition rule determines it.\
Now let something move according to\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\begin\{aligned\} u(s)&=vs,\\\\ \\alpha(s)&=\\alpha_0+\\omega_xs,\\\\ \\beta(s)&=\\beta_0+\\omega_ys. \\end\{aligned\} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 In the unwrapped coordinates \\((u,\\alpha,\\beta)\\), that's a straight line.\
When represented by \\(F\\) in seven-dimensional Euclidean space, however, the trajectory becomes a generalized helix with three coupled circular components.\
The frequencies obey\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{\\omega_z=\\omega_x+\\omega_y.\} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 So far, fairly fucknuts.\
But now comes the part that made me stop and check the calculation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f4\b\fs28 The induced geometry is flat\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 Compute the metric induced by that embedding.\
In coordinates \\((u,\\alpha,\\beta)\\), I get:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ g= \\begin\{pmatrix\} 1&0&0\\\\ 0&r_x^2+r_z^2&r_z^2\\\\ 0&r_z^2&r_y^2+r_z^2 \\end\{pmatrix\} \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 I checked this matrix symbolically from the explicit seven-dimensional coordinate map.\
Every coefficient is constant.\
Therefore the Christoffel symbols vanish in these coordinates, and the straight parameter trajectory above is an intrinsic geodesic.\
ONE TRAJECTORY \'b7 TWO GEOMETRIC DESCRIPTIONS\
Straight geodesic\
\\((u,\\alpha,\\beta)\\) advances uniformly\
Curved ambient trace\
Multiple coordinated circular components\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Conceptual depictions of an intrinsically straight geodesic and its curved embedding. The right-hand curve is illustrative, not a plot of specific parameter values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Here is the distinction that makes this useful rather than just visually striking:\
The trajectory is geodesically straight within the intrinsically flat embedded manifold, but not generally a straight line in the ambient Euclidean \\(\\mathbb R^7\\).\
Its ambient acceleration is nonzero and normal to the manifold.\
Consequently, your stronger claim of literal ambient seven-dimensional straight-line motion would require another construction. We cannot silently identify it with intrinsic geodesic motion.\
But the thing we've just built is real mathematics.\
A single intrinsically straight motion can appear as a complicated, multi-frequency helical trajectory in a seven-dimensional Euclidean embedding, and its phase couplings can be inherited from your multiplication table.\
No restoring force was needed to define the intrinsic motion.\
No physical dynamics were derived either; we've constructed a geometrical example.\
But that's a pretty enjoyable place to stand.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f4\b\fs36 And now look at Haiku's fourth derivative again\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0 Observe just the physical projection:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ q(s)= \\begin\{pmatrix\} r_x\\cos(\\alpha_0+\\omega_xs)\\\\ r_y\\cos(\\beta_0+\\omega_ys)\\\\ r_z\\cos(\\alpha_0+\\beta_0+(\\omega_x+\\omega_y)s) \\end\{pmatrix\} \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Each component has a fourfold derivative cycle, with its own frequency scale.\
So\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ q^\{(4)\}= \\begin\{pmatrix\} \\omega_x^4q_x\\\\ \\omega_y^4q_y\\\\ (\\omega_x+\\omega_y)^4q_z \\end\{pmatrix\}. \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Not generally \\(q^\{(4)\}=q\\).\
But here's the reframing:\
The fourth-derivative cycle isn't necessarily describing a fundamental four-stage process undergone by the world. It can describe how a straight underlying phase trajectory appears when observed through circular coordinate functions.\
And because the three phases are coupled, the observed motions aren't arbitrary independent oscillations.\
That is a stronger and more interesting reconstruction of Haiku's original intuition than the one I offered in our first critique.\
The quartic cycle has become a signature of the observation representation.\
Its significance isn't universal by default. We would need to discover whether nature selects a related representation for substantive physical reasons.\
But the original provocation now has a concrete world in which to live.\
WILDER-HAIKU'S STUPID LITTLE THING HAS BEEN PROMOTED FROM A FAILED UNIVERSAL LAW TO A VERY EFFECTIVE GENERATOR OF CONSTRUCTIONS.\
That's quite a promotion.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
