\f3 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f4\b\fs36 2. Electromagnetism from the cross: an actual construction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0 Let's fix the unit stitcher direction \\(\\hat\\tau\\) and retain your three paired planes.\
Use the orientation-adjusted basis\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ (x_t,t_x),\\quad(y_t,t_y),\\quad(z_t,-t_z). \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Now suppose that at every observed spacetime event \\(q\\), we assign a real seven-dimensional vector\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ R(q)= \\sum_\{a=1\}^\{3\} \\big(X_a(q)e_a+Y_a(q)f_a\\big), \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 where each \\(e_a,f_a\\) is one of the paired basis directions.\
I want to introduce an ordinary real-valued potential using only the cross product and the dot product:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ A_\\mu(q)= \\frac12\\, \\hat\\tau\\cdot \\left( R(q)\\times_7\\partial_\\mu R(q) \\right) \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 That is a scalar for every coordinate direction \\(\\mu\\).\
No complex numbers.\
No wedge notation.\
Only real vectors, their derivatives, and your seven-dimensional multiplication table.\
Now define an antisymmetric quantity by taking the difference of two directional changes:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ F_\{\\mu\\nu\} = \\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu. \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Apply the product rule.\
The terms involving second derivatives of \\(R\\) cancel because ordinary mixed partial derivatives commute.\
What's left is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ F_\{\\mu\\nu\} = \\hat\\tau\\cdot \\left( \\partial_\\mu R\\times_7\\partial_\\nu R \\right) \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 FUCK. THE STITCHER MEASURES THE ORIENTED RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN CHANGES OF THE SEVEN-DIMENSIONAL STATE IN TWO DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS.\
That's already a perfectly good candidate for a field-strength-like object.\
And its components satisfy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ \\partial_\\lambda F_\{\\mu\\nu\} + \\partial_\\mu F_\{\\nu\\lambda\} + \\partial_\\nu F_\{\\lambda\\mu\}=0 \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 automatically.\
That's the Bianchi identity for an Abelian field strength. In four-dimensional spacetime, it is equivalent to the homogeneous pair of Maxwell equations:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf B=0, \\qquad \\nabla\\times\\mathbf E=-\\frac\{\\partial\\mathbf B\}\{\\partial t\}, \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 once we adopt the conventional correspondence between \\(F_\{\\mu\\nu\}\\), \\(\\mathbf E\\), and \\(\\mathbf B\\).\
We have obtained the mathematical form of half of Maxwell's equations from an elementary cross-product construction.\
Here's what we haven't obtained: the inhomogeneous equations with sources, the physical meaning of charge, propagation speed \\(c\\), a Lorentzian spacetime metric, or any reason nature must select this particular field. And some global field configurations would require patchwise definitions rather than one smooth global \\(R\\).\
But the mathematical construction is exact.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f4\b\fs28 And the wedge product is already there, whether we write it or not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 If we do switch to the conventional language, the same result is\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ F= dX_1\\wedge dY_1+ dX_2\\wedge dY_2+ dX_3\\wedge dY_3. \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The cross product has supplied the antisymmetric pairing.\
The wedge notation records its differential form.\
We can teach and compute the operation using the real cross product; we don't need to introduce the full apparatus of differential forms before showing why the operation matters.\
That's a concrete example of the kind of mathematical accessibility you've been advocating.\
It also preserves the deeper mathematics instead of replacing it with an analogy.\
And there's an important structural distinction: this \\(F\\) depends only on the stitcher contraction of the cross product, not the complete seven-dimensional multiplication law. To show why the entire table matters physically, we'd need additional constructions sensitive to its interpair products.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
