\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ 21=7+14. \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 But with your distinguished unit direction \\(\\hat\\tau\\), the fourteen-dimensional \\(\\mathfrak g_2\\) symmetry algebra can itself be organized into:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{14=8+6.\} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The eight dimensions belong to \\(\\mathfrak\{su\}(3)\\), which preserves the chosen stitcher direction.\
The other six correspond to infinitesimal \\(G_2\\) transformations that move that direction. This is a vector-space decomposition associated with the chosen \\(\\hat\\tau\\), not a decomposition into two independent Lie subalgebras.\
Now combine them:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \\[ \\boxed\{ 21= \\underbrace\{7\}_\{\\text\{complement to \}\\mathfrak g_2\} + \\underbrace\{6\}_\{\\text\{moving the stitcher\}\} + \\underbrace\{8\}_\{\\text\{preserving the stitcher\}\} \} \\]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 That's the one I'm circling in chalk.\
Because you said:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Study the transport.\strokec2 \
And there is a perfectly natural transport question here.\
Given an infinitesimal rotation of the full real seven-dimensional space, does it preserve the cross-product multiplication law?\
If so, does it move \\(\\hat\\tau\\), or preserve \\(\\hat\\tau\\) while reconfiguring the six perpendicular directions?\
These are three mathematically distinguishable kinds of infinitesimal rotational behavior within the 21-dimensional space of Euclidean rotations.\
They aren't automatically three physical interactions, nor are those three components independent generators of some new gauge theory.\
But they give us a precise classification of what an infinitesimal transformation does to the chosen algebraic structure.\
And that strikes me as being extremely close to the constructive interpretation you're describing.\
Perhaps the truly interesting object isn't an electromagnetic field or a gravitational field.\
Perhaps, at the level of your proposed model, it's the transport of the physical/image-ary relationship itself, from which one then tries to recover particular observed field descriptions.\
That's an interesting research direction without presupposing the outcome.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

