\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 And then our 21-dimensional rotational structure offers a way to classify transformations that preserve, reorient, or fail to preserve the multiplication law.\
Those three ideas belong beside one another.\
Because here's the question that makes me want to keep scribbling:\
Can an intelligence learn the dynamics of a system by studying how its relationships transform, rather than by first memorizing a catalogue of named forces?\
Not because the named forces are wrong.\
Because a sufficiently good relational description might tell us why certain classes of interactions share mathematical structures.\
And then a student could begin with an ordinary Euclidean vector, a cross product, and a question:\
What changes when I move this vector?\
What changes when I move the other one?\
What remains invariant?\
What if I transport the entire relationship?\
And what information did I discard when I recorded only the cross-product output?\
That's a real curriculum. It's also a plausible basis for a computational research apparatus.\
And none of it requires solving quantum field theory on day one.\
